module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Kaehler.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.Basic
public import Sheaf.RingTheory.ContinuousKaehlerDifferential

/-!
# Reconstruction from shifted differential truncations

Source: Stacks Project, algebra.tex, Tags 02HQ (`lemma-differential-mod-power-ideal`)
and 00RO (`lemma-universal-omega`), with the agent auxiliary reconstruction argument
(blueprint: lem-trf-complete-derivation-truncation-reconstruction).

The index `n` denotes the positive truncation exponent `r = n + 1`. Mathlib's
completion also includes exponent zero, whose module quotient is zero.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

namespace KaehlerDifferential

/-- A complete module generated by a derivation is reconstructed from compatible maps
into the differentials of the adic quotients. The shifted maps are uniquely determined
on universal differential symbols; their inverse-limit map is inverse to the given maps.

Source: Stacks Project, algebra.tex, Tags 02HQ and 00RO; the full reconstruction is an
agent auxiliary argument (blueprint: lem-trf-complete-derivation-truncation-reconstruction).

Proof:
1. Expand the derivation of a product of `n + 2` ideal elements by Leibniz. Every
coefficient lies in the `(n + 1)`st power, giving the first assertion by additivity.
2. Reduce the derivation modulo that power of the module. Step 1 makes it descend
from `R` to the ring modulo the `(n + 2)`nd power. Its module action factors through
that ring. The universal property of Kähler differentials (Tag 00RO,
`def-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials`) gives the unique linear map `η n`.
3. On each generator `δ (x i)`, the composite `η n ∘ φ (n + 1)` is the module
quotient map. Spanning and linearity extend this identity to all of `N`, including
an empty generating family, when `N` is zero.
4. Linearity and annihilation of the target by the `(n + 1)`st ideal power make
`φ n` descend to `φbar n`. On differential symbols `φbar n ∘ η n` is the quotient
transition. These symbols span the source, so the maps agree everywhere.
5. The composites of `η (n + 1)` with module reduction and of the differential
transition with `η n` agree on every differential symbol, hence everywhere.
Consequently applying `η n` to component `n + 1` of a compatible differential tuple
gives a compatible module tuple. Adjoin its unique degree-zero component to obtain
`q : AdicCompletion m N`.
6. Componentwise operations (`def-trf-continuous-kahler-differentials`) give the
linear map `Φ` with coordinates `φ n`. Define `Ψ` by applying the inverse of the
canonical completion isomorphism to `q`. Step 3 proves `Ψ ∘ Φ = id` after applying
the injective completion map. For the other identity, its component `n` is
`φbar n (η n (c.val (n + 1)))`, which by Step 4 is the transition applied to that
component, hence `c.val n`. Equality of tuples proves `Φ ∘ Ψ = id`.
-/
theorem completeDerivationTruncationReconstruction
    (k : Type u) (R : Type v) (N : Type w)
    [CommRing k] [CommRing R] [Algebra k R]
    [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] [Module k N] [IsScalarTower k R N]
    (m : Ideal R)
    (hcomplete : Function.Bijective (AdicCompletion.of m N))
    (δ : Derivation k R N) (d : ℕ) (x : Fin d → R)
    (hgen : Submodule.span R (Set.range (fun i : Fin d ↦ δ (x i))) = ⊤)
    (φ : ∀ n : ℕ, N →ₗ[R] KaehlerDifferential k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 1)))
    (hcompat : ∀ n : ℕ, (adicTransition k R m n).comp (φ (n + 1)) = φ n)
    (hφ : ∀ (n : ℕ) (a : R),
      φ n (δ a) = D k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 1)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ a)) :
    (∀ (n : ℕ) (a : R), a ∈ m ^ (n + 2) →
      δ a ∈ (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N)) ∧
    ∃ η : ∀ n : ℕ, KaehlerDifferential k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 2)) →ₗ[R]
        N ⧸ (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N),
      (∀ (n : ℕ) (a : R),
        η n (D k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 2)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ a)) =
          (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N).mkQ (δ a)) ∧
      (∀ (n : ℕ) (f : KaehlerDifferential k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 2)) →ₗ[R]
          N ⧸ (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N)),
        (∀ a : R, f (D k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 2)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ a)) =
          (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N).mkQ (δ a)) → f = η n) ∧
      (∀ n : ℕ,
        (Submodule.factorPowSucc m N (n + 1)).comp (η (n + 1)) =
          (η n).comp (adicTransition k R m (n + 1))) ∧
      (∀ n : ℕ, (η n).comp (φ (n + 1)) =
        (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N).mkQ) ∧
      ∃ φbar : ∀ n : ℕ, (N ⧸ (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N)) →ₗ[R]
          KaehlerDifferential k (R ⧸ m ^ (n + 1)),
        (∀ n : ℕ, (φbar n).comp (m ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R N).mkQ = φ n) ∧
        (∀ n : ℕ, (φbar n).comp (η n) = adicTransition k R m n) ∧
        ∃ (Φ : N →ₗ[R] Continuous k R m) (Ψ : Continuous k R m →ₗ[R] N),
          (∀ (z : N) (n : ℕ), (Φ z).val n = φ n z) ∧
          (∀ c : Continuous k R m, ∃ q : AdicCompletion m N,
            (∀ n : ℕ, q.val (n + 1) = η n (c.val (n + 1))) ∧
            Ψ c = (LinearEquiv.ofBijective (AdicCompletion.of m N) hcomplete).symm q) ∧
          Ψ.comp Φ = LinearMap.id ∧ Φ.comp Ψ = LinearMap.id := by
  sorry

end KaehlerDifferential
